Chapter 5: Pilot Validation Protocol

5.1 Designation

This protocol is a pilot, not a confirmatory trial. Its objectives are feasibility, acceptability, safety, preliminary effect estimation, variance estimation, and parameter calibration for a future Phase II trial.

5.2 Design

A crossover randomized pilot with three conditions:

1. AHDS session (7–15 minutes, adaptive missions).
2. Passive break (same duration, no structured activity).
3. Physical recovery (outdoor walk, same duration).

The third condition is not a neutral control. It combines movement, environmental change, natural light, potential conversation, and separation from the workstation. It is an active recovery condition. The scientific question is therefore:

Does collective ludic decoupling add anything beyond stopping work and moving?

Participants (N = 30) complete all three conditions in randomized order, with a minimum 48-hour washout between conditions.

5.3 Measures

· Primary. Pre/post change in perceived stress (visual analog scale) and affective state (PANAS short form).
· Secondary. Psychological safety (Edmondson 3-item scale), cognitive load (NASA-TLX single item), willingness to repeat.
· Behavioral. Acoustic activity index, movement density, spatial dispersion.
· Qualitative. Post-session open-ended item on perceived humiliation or discomfort (0–10 scale).

5.4 Statistical Model

Because participants are nested within groups, the primary analysis uses a linear mixed model:

Y_{ijk} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{Condition}_k + u_{\text{group}(j)} + u_{\text{participant}(i)} + \varepsilon_{ijk}

with random intercepts for group and participant. Carryover effects are assessed by including condition order as a covariate.

We will estimate the intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) to quantify group clustering. We will report 95% confidence intervals for effect sizes rather than p-values, consistent with pilot trial guidelines. A power analysis using variance estimates from this pilot informs the Phase II sample size. The 48-hour washout may be insufficient to fully dissipate psychological safety carryover effects; this is acknowledged as a limitation of the pilot design and will be addressed in the Phase II protocol.

5.5 Hypotheses

Central hypothesis. Short-duration collective activities produce greater subjective decompression when they maximize voluntary cognitive decoupling from work-related modes while minimizing social evaluation, performance demands, physical risk, and informational surveillance.

Sub-hypotheses:

· H_1: AHDS > passive break on subjective decompression.
· H_2: AHDS is not necessarily superior to physical recovery on all parameters.
· H_3: Higher perceived autonomy \rightarrow higher decompression.
· H_4: Evaluation pressure \rightarrow lower decompression.
· H_5 (negative hypothesis): Laughter \not\equiv decompression.

The last is explicitly falsifiable: if acoustic activity correlates strongly with decompression regardless of context, the framework's central claim is undermined.

