Appendix A: Mathematical Proofs and Derivations

A.1 Preliminaries and Notation

Throughout these appendices, we adopt the following notation:

· \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n denotes the cognitive–social–physical state space, with n = 10 dimensions as specified in Chapter 3.
· \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{X} denotes a state vector.
· W \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n} denotes a positive-definite weighting matrix.
· \|\mathbf{v}\|_W = \sqrt{\mathbf{v}^\top W \mathbf{v}} denotes the weighted Euclidean norm.
· \mathcal{W}, \mathcal{D}, \mathcal{R} \subset \mathcal{X} denote the work, decompression, and recovery regions respectively.
· S, A, O denote the POMDP state, action, and observation spaces.
· b_t(s) denotes the belief state at time t.

A.2 Proof that the Cognitive Decoupling Distance is a Metric

Definition A.1 (Cognitive Decoupling Distance). For \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} \in \mathbb{R}^n, the Cognitive Decoupling Distance is defined as:

D_C(\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}) = \|\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}\|_W = \sqrt{(\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}})^\top W (\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}})}

Theorem A.1. D_C is a metric on \mathbb{R}^n.

Proof. We verify the four metric axioms.

(1) Non-negativity. Since W is positive-definite, for any \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^n, \mathbf{v}^\top W \mathbf{v} \ge 0, with equality if and only if \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}. Therefore D_C(\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}) \ge 0.

(2) Identity of indiscernibles. D_C(\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}) = 0 \iff \|\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}\|_W = 0 \iff \mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} = \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}, by positive-definiteness of W.

(3) Symmetry. D_C(\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}) = \|\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}\|_W = \|(-1)(\mathbf{x}_{\text{work}} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}})\|_W = |-1| \cdot \|\mathbf{x}_{\text{work}} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}}\|_W = D_C(\mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}}).

(4) Triangle inequality. Since W is positive-definite, it admits a Cholesky decomposition W = L^\top L where L is upper triangular with positive diagonal entries. Then:

\|\mathbf{v}\|_W = \sqrt{\mathbf{v}^\top W \mathbf{v}} = \sqrt{\mathbf{v}^\top L^\top L \mathbf{v}} = \sqrt{(L\mathbf{v})^\top (L\mathbf{v})} = \|L\mathbf{v}\|_2

where \|\cdot\|_2 is the standard Euclidean norm. Therefore:

D_C(\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}) = \|L(\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}})\|_2

For any three points \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^n:

D_C(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{z}) = \|L(\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{z})\|_2 = \|L(\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y}) + L(\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{z})\|_2 \le \|L(\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y})\|_2 + \|L(\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{z})\|_2 = D_C(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) + D_C(\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{z})

by the triangle inequality for \|\cdot\|_2. \square

A.3 Proof of Non-Emptiness of the Optimal Decoupling Band

Definition A.2 (Optimal Decoupling Band). The optimal decoupling band is the interval [D_{\min}, D_{\max}] where:

D_{\min} = \min_{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{D}} D_C(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}})

D_{\max} = \max_{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{D}} D_C(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}})

Theorem A.2. If \mathcal{D} is non-empty and compact, then [D_{\min}, D_{\max}] is non-empty.

Proof. Since \mathcal{D} is non-empty, there exists at least one \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{D}. The function f(\mathbf{x}) = D_C(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}) is continuous (since D_C is a metric, hence continuous in its arguments). A continuous function on a non-empty compact set attains its minimum and maximum. Therefore D_{\min} and D_{\max} exist and are finite, and [D_{\min}, D_{\max}] is non-empty. \square

Corollary A.2.1. If \mathcal{D} is non-empty and compact, then for any \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}} \in \mathcal{X}, there exists at least one mission \mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} \in \mathcal{D} such that D_C(\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}) \in [D_{\min}, D_{\max}].

A.4 Proof of the Radical Voluntarism Theorem

Definition A.3 (Participatory Freedom). Let V_p \in [0, 1] denote the level of participatory freedom. V_p = 1 if and only if every participant can withdraw at every moment without cost.

Definition A.4 (Withdrawal Cost). \mathrm{Cost}_{\text{withdrawal}} = 0 if and only if withdrawal does not trigger social sanction, managerial notification, data retention, participation tracking at the individual level, or explanation requirements.

Theorem A.3 (Radical Voluntarism). V_p = 1 \iff \mathrm{Cost}_{\text{withdrawal}} = 0.

Proof.

(⇒) Suppose V_p = 1. By Definition A.3, every participant can withdraw at every moment without cost. By Definition A.4, this is equivalent to \mathrm{Cost}_{\text{withdrawal}} = 0.

(⇐) Suppose \mathrm{Cost}_{\text{withdrawal}} = 0. By Definition A.4, withdrawal does not trigger social sanction, managerial notification, data retention, participation tracking, or explanation requirements. Therefore every participant can withdraw at every moment without cost, so V_p = 1. \square

Corollary A.3.1. In the AHDS, V_p = 1 is an architectural property, not a policy statement, because \mathrm{Cost}_{\text{withdrawal}} = 0 is enforced by the privacy firewall and data destruction protocols.

A.5 Proof of the Non-Instrumentalization Compatibility Theorem

Definition A.5 (Non-Instrumentalization). An intervention is non-instrumentalized relative to an organization if:

I_{\text{org}} = 0

where I_{\text{org}} denotes the degree to which session outcomes are used by the organization for performance evaluation, compensation, promotion, disciplinary action, productivity ranking, or attendance ranking.

Theorem A.4 (Compatibility of Non-Instrumentalization with System Objective). I_{\text{org}} = 0 is compatible with the system having an internal objective.

Proof. Let \mathcal{O}_{\text{sys}} denote the system's internal objective. We show that I_{\text{org}} = 0 does not imply \mathcal{O}_{\text{sys}} = \emptyset.

The system's internal objective may be:

\mathcal{O}_{\text{sys}} = \text{facilitate voluntary transition into } \mathcal{D}

This objective is defined entirely in terms of the system's internal dynamics and the participants' voluntary choices. It does not require any outcome data to be transmitted to the organization. The privacy firewall ensures that no individual or small-group data is accessible to management (Section 4.10). The data destruction protocol ensures that no persistent records survive the session. Therefore I_{\text{org}} = 0 while \mathcal{O}_{\text{sys}} \neq \emptyset. \square

Corollary A.4.1. The apparent paradox—that a system can optimize the conditions for decompression without instrumentally optimizing the decompressed person—is resolved: the system optimizes the possibility of decompression, not the decompressed person.

A.6 Derivation of the POMDP Belief Update

Theorem A.5 (Belief State Update). The belief state b_t(s) = P(s_t = s \mid o_{1:t}, a_{1:t-1}) satisfies the recursive Bayes filter:

b_t(s') = \frac{\Omega(o_t \mid s') \sum_{s \in S} P(s' \mid s, a_{t-1}) b_{t-1}(s)}{\sum_{s'' \in S} \Omega(o_t \mid s'') \sum_{s \in S} P(s'' \mid s, a_{t-1}) b_{t-1}(s)}

Proof. By Bayes' rule:

b_t(s') = P(s_t = s' \mid o_{1:t}, a_{1:t-1}) = \frac{P(o_t \mid s_t = s', o_{1:t-1}, a_{1:t-1}) P(s_t = s' \mid o_{1:t-1}, a_{1:t-1})}{P(o_t \mid o_{1:t-1}, a_{1:t-1})}

By the Markov property and the definition of the observation function:

P(o_t \mid s_t = s', o_{1:t-1}, a_{1:t-1}) = \Omega(o_t \mid s')

By the law of total probability:

P(s_t = s' \mid o_{1:t-1}, a_{1:t-1}) = \sum_{s \in S} P(s_t = s' \mid s_{t-1} = s, a_{t-1}) P(s_{t-1} = s \mid o_{1:t-1}, a_{1:t-2})

= \sum_{s \in S} P(s' \mid s, a_{t-1}) b_{t-1}(s)

The denominator is the normalizing constant:

P(o_t \mid o_{1:t-1}, a_{1:t-1}) = \sum_{s'' \in S} \Omega(o_t \mid s'') \sum_{s \in S} P(s'' \mid s, a_{t-1}) b_{t-1}(s)

Combining these yields the result. \square

A.7 Proof of the Lexicographic Constrained Optimization Solution Concept

Definition A.6 (Lexicographic Constrained Optimization). Let \mathbf{Y} = [Y_1, \ldots, Y_m] be a vector objective and let \mathcal{C} = \{c_1, \ldots, c_k\} be a set of hard constraints. The lexicographic constrained optimization problem is:

\text{lexmax} \; \mathbf{Y} \quad \text{subject to} \quad c_i \le c_i^{\max} \; \forall i

where lexmax denotes lexicographic maximization: maximize Y_1, then Y_2, etc., subject to the constraints.

Theorem A.6 (Well-Definedness). If the feasible set \mathcal{F} = \{\mathbf{x} : c_i(\mathbf{x}) \le c_i^{\max} \; \forall i\} is non-empty and compact, and each Y_j is continuous, then the lexicographic constrained optimization problem has a solution.

Proof. We proceed by induction on the number of objectives m.

Base case (m = 1): Maximize Y_1 subject to \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{F}. Since \mathcal{F} is non-empty and compact and Y_1 is continuous, the extreme value theorem guarantees that Y_1 attains its maximum on \mathcal{F}. Let \mathcal{F}_1 = \arg\max_{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{F}} Y_1(\mathbf{x}). \mathcal{F}_1 is non-empty and compact.

Inductive step: Suppose we have solved for objectives 1, \ldots, j-1, obtaining feasible set \mathcal{F}_{j-1}. Maximize Y_j subject to \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{F}_{j-1}. Since \mathcal{F}_{j-1} is non-empty and compact and Y_j is continuous, the extreme value theorem guarantees a solution. Let \mathcal{F}_j = \arg\max_{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{F}_{j-1}} Y_j(\mathbf{x}).

After m steps, \mathcal{F}_m is non-empty, and any \mathbf{x}^* \in \mathcal{F}_m is a solution to the lexicographic constrained optimization problem. \square

Corollary A.6.1. In the AHDS, the constraints H \le H_{\max} and C \le C_{\max} are hard, and the system satisfices \Delta V and \Delta A while minimizing P(\text{withdrawal}). No scalarization is performed.

A.8 Derivation of the Absurdity Engine's Constraint Satisfaction

Definition A.7 (Mission Feasibility). A mission \mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} \in \mathcal{X} is feasible if:

1. D_C(\mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{work}}) \in [D_{\min}, D_{\max}]
2. \mathbf{x}_{\text{mission}} satisfies all six constraints in Section 4.5.

Theorem A.7 (Existence of Feasible Missions). If \mathcal{D} is non-empty and compact and the constraint set is non-empty, then there exists at least one feasible mission.

Proof. By Theorem A.2, [D_{\min}, D_{\max}] is non-empty. By assumption, the constraint set is non-empty. The intersection of a non-empty compact set with a non-empty closed set is non-empty and compact. Therefore there exists at least one feasible mission. \square

