Appendix D: Sensor Architecture and Privacy Firewall

D.1 Low-Resolution Infrared Array Specification

The movement density sensor is a low-resolution infrared array with the following specifications:

Parameter Value
Resolution 8 × 8 pixels
Wavelength 8–14 μm (long-wave infrared)
Field of view 60° × 60°
Frame rate 10 Hz
Output Temperature matrix (64 values)
Processing On-edge, no transmission
Data retention None (discarded after feature extraction)

The movement density \mu_{\text{mouv}}(t) is computed as:

\mu_{\text{mouv}}(t) = \frac{1}{64} \sum_{i=1}^{8} \sum_{j=1}^{8} \mathbb{1}\left[ |T_{ij}(t) - T_{ij}(t-1)| > \tau \right]

where T_{ij}(t) is the temperature at pixel (i, j) at time t, and \tau is a threshold (typically 0.5°C).

D.2 Acoustic Processing Pipeline

The acoustic processing pipeline is specified as follows:

```
Raw Audio → RMS dB → Temporal Variance → A_act
```

Step 1: RMS dB Computation

\text{RMS}(t) = \sqrt{\frac{1}{N} \sum_{n=1}^{N} x_n^2}

\text{dB}(t) = 20 \log_{10}(\text{RMS}(t))

Step 2: Temporal Variance

\text{Var}(t) = \frac{1}{W} \sum_{k=t-W}^{t} (\text{dB}(k) - \overline{\text{dB}})^2

where W is a window size (typically 1 second).

Step 3: Acoustic Activity Index

A_{\text{act}}(t) = \sigma\left( \alpha \cdot \text{dB}(t) + \beta \cdot \text{Var}(t) \right)

where \sigma is the sigmoid function, and \alpha, \beta are scaling parameters.

Privacy Note: No spectral identification, no voice ID, no affect inference is performed. Only energy and temporal variance are computed.

D.3 Spatial Dispersion Computation

The spatial dispersion \Delta_{\text{disp}}(t) is computed from the infrared array as:

\Delta_{\text{disp}}(t) = \frac{1}{64} \sum_{i=1}^{8} \sum_{j=1}^{8} \left( \frac{T_{ij}(t) - \overline{T}(t)}{\sigma_T(t)} \right)^2

where \overline{T}(t) is the mean temperature and \sigma_T(t) is the standard deviation.

