E.4 Statistical Analysis Plan

Primary Analysis:

Y_{ijk} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{Condition}_k + u_{\text{group}(j)} + u_{\text{participant}(i)} + \varepsilon_{ijk}

where:

· Y_{ijk} is the outcome for participant i in group j under condition k
· \beta_0 is the intercept
· \beta_1 is the fixed effect of condition
· u_{\text{group}(j)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{\text{group}}) is the random effect of group
· u_{\text{participant}(i)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{\text{participant}}) is the random effect of participant
· \varepsilon_{ijk} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_\varepsilon) is the residual error

ICC Estimation:

\text{ICC} = \frac{\sigma^2_{\text{group}}}{\sigma^2_{\text{group}} + \sigma^2_{\text{participant}} + \sigma^2_\varepsilon}

Effect Size Reporting:

We will report 95% confidence intervals for effect sizes rather than p-values, consistent with pilot trial guidelines.

Carryover Effects:

We will include condition order as a covariate:

Y_{ijk} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{Condition}_k + \beta_2 \text{Order}_k + u_{\text{group}(j)} + u_{\text{participant}(i)} + \varepsilon_{ijk}

E.5 Washout Period Justification

The 48-hour washout period is chosen based on:

1. Psychological safety carryover: May persist beyond 48 hours; acknowledged as a limitation.
2. Affective carryover: Typically dissipates within 24 hours.
3. Cognitive load carryover: Typically dissipates within 2 hours.
4. Practical constraints: Longer washout periods increase dropout risk.

